module effect_e_name.neg_1

// `Effect[T]` (D87/D142, the builtin generic type of a handler's value) is a
// known type word (2026-09-21) and, since the E4 wave (274.11), a SEEDED builtin
// type -- so this program is no longer refused as "a type novac does not know".
//
// WHY THIS FIXTURE, THEN AND NOW. It was written to pin that `Effect[T]` in a
// parameter or return position does not LIE ("undeclared type name `Effect`"):
// the flow reached the honest subset refusal about an unknown type shape. The
// E4 wave seeded `Effect`, and the refusal disappeared -- and the fixture went
// red with NO diagnostic at all, which is what exposed registry 221.1 #1309:
// the program is not legal Nova. `x` is a `ro` parameter (no `mut`, D176) and
// `-> Effect[int]` is not `ro`, which the oracle refuses as E_READONLY_COERCE
// (D246 amendment); novac accepted the same shape for every non-scalar type,
// `[]int` included, long before the wave.
//
// So the contract moved twice, and the fixture follows both: the name is
// known (no "undeclared"), and the program is refused for the reason the
// language gives. The general door is pinned by novac/fixtures/ro_param_return/.

// NOVAC_EXPECT cannot return `x` through a result that is not `ro`

fn pass_through(x Effect[int]) -> Effect[int] => x

fn main() {
    println(1)
}
